    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense from an originalist perspective. But if we look at the other side, what are the main criticisms of that approach? Some people argue that focusing so heavily on 18th-century intent is basically just a way to avoid addressing contemporary public safety issues. How do living constitutionalists respond to the idea that originalism is too rigid for a modern society?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Living constitutionalists argue that strict adherence to 18th-century context ignores the Constitution's inherent flaws and the necessity of adapting fundamental rights to changing societal needs and technological advancements. Critics of originalism further contend that it often prioritizes textualism over thorough historical analysis, leading to conclusions that may read the Founder's intent against the common understanding of the text itself. While supporters claim this rigidity ensures stability, opponents point to the reality that modern public safety issues require judicial flexibility to balance individual liberties against evolving dangers that the Framers could not have foreseen. Ultimately, the debate centers on whether the Constitution's enduring legitimacy relies on its unchanging text or its capacity to serve as a dynamic guide for contemporary governance.",
